These docs are kept in four parts, and each answers a different question.

## What skoro is

Two tracks say what the library is today, and are rewritten whenever it changes.

- **The [guide](../../guide/) is top-down.** It starts from what a user writes (a model, a
  distribution, a density to evaluate) and reaches into the machinery only as far as an example
  needs. Read it to use skoro.
- **The [concepts](../../concepts/) are bottom-up.** They build the library from its primitive
  objects upward, each idea resting only on the ones before it. Read them to understand why skoro
  works the way it does, or before changing it.

The [reference](../../reference/) holds what is looked up rather than read: the crates, the
glossary, the notation.

## What it rests on

The [mathematics](../../math/) is bottom-up too, and independent of skoro. It holds every piece
of mathematics the pages rely on that would be true if the library did not exist, one idea to a
note: a retraction, the normal distribution on a curved space, the Laplace approximation. The notes
stand in an order, in three parts: spaces and their charts, probability on those spaces, and least
squares with the Gaussian approximations built on it. Each note rests only on the notes before it,
so that read from the first to the last they tell the whole of the mathematics once. A page links
the notes it needs, and a note says which earlier notes it builds on, so a reader who arrives in
the middle knows how far back to go. The map on that page draws the whole network, together with
the archive's documents that use it.

What is skoro's own, a choice among true things or a statement about something the library
defines, is not in the notes. It is in the concepts, which say what it is, and in the archive,
which argued for it.

## How it came to be

The [archive](../../archive/) keeps the record of every piece of development. A feature's entry
holds the mathematics worked out for it, the implementation companion that turned the mathematics
into a design, and the plan it was built by, each kept as it was approved. A decision's entry holds
the argument for it.

The archive is history, and history is not rewritten. One thing in a finished entry may change:
where a note it refers to has since been rewritten, the reference is brought up to date, and the
entry says which later entry did so. Where the concepts track _tells_ you what is,
an entry _discusses_ what might be, as it stood when it was written. When the library later
changes, a new entry revises the old one, and the old one says so at its top. Every page lists the
entries that made it what it is, under "How this came to be", and every entry lists the pages it
changed.

> [!NOTE]
> Every document is also served as Markdown (follow "View as Markdown" at its foot), and
> [llms.txt](../../llms.txt) indexes all of them for language models.

## Where to go next

Pick the track that matches what you came for. Each card opens the track's first page, and every
page ends by pointing to the next.

